                          LEARNING TO HOLD



    Four seconds.
    Then a delivery bike chimed outside the window, and my attention
went to it like a dog to a squirrel, and the whole café slid out of my
hands.
    “Again,” said Rafa.
    I did it again. Five seconds. A baby’s squeal. Gone.
    “Again.”
    The fourth time, I caught myself gritting my teeth, white-knuckled
on the counter, sweating. Holding it the way you hold your breath.
Rafa watched me do it with an expression of mild, patient regret.
    “You’re doing it like them,” he said.
    “Doing what?”
    “Squeezing. A flashlight. You think you hold a thing by gripping it.”
He took a pencil out of his coat pocket, a stub, yellow, chewed, and slid
it across the counter. “Don’t grip. Do something else with your hands.
Anything. Hold the room with the part of you that doesn’t have any‐
thing else to do.”
    I looked at the pencil.
    Then I did the thing I’d been doing since I was six. I tapped it. Quiet,
small, on the counter, in time with nothing in particular.
    The café held.
    Nine people in a café and the El and the grinder and the baby, all of
it slid into its track and stayed. I tapped the pencil. The delivery bike
went by again, and I heard it, and I didn’t chase it. I let it be one more
thing in the room. A minute went by. I didn’t notice it going.
    “Poeta.” Mr. Rodríguez had appeared at my elbow with a rag.
“You’re tapping a pencil on my counter. It’s been four minutes.” He
lowered his voice. “The whole café is breathing at the same time. It’s
making me nervous.”
    Rafa took a sip of his cortado. It was cold. He didn’t seem to mind.
    “Twenty-five years,” he said. “You’ve been fighting your hand for
twenty-five years. Your hand knows what it’s doing.”
    He held up four fingers and counted them off, which I should have
found funnier than I did. “You hear what’s under a room. You can hold
